      - title: What the Real AI Attacks of the Last Two Years Actually Taught Us
        description: "Many AI security talks deal in \"an attacker could.\" This one deals only in what actually happened.\r\n\r\nIn the last two years, real AI systems in production have leaked private data, deleted databases, been tricked through documents they were just supposed to read, and had their own connected tools turned against them. These are disclosed incidents and CVEs, not hypotheticals. And when you line them up, patterns emerge.\r\n\r\nThis talk walks the real breaches that occurred in AI systems and pulls out the the common threads underneath: the same few root causes, over and over, in systems built by people who thought they'd been careful. \r\n\r\nI'll show what happened, why, and the handful of mistakes that keep repeating, so you can recognise them in your own stack before they're disclosed for you.\r\n\r\nIf you want security for AI grounded in evidence, come see what the actual attacks can teach us."
        speakers:
          - Liana Anca Tomescu
        is_break: false
        start: "10:25:00"
        end: "11:10:00"
      - title: Morning break
        description: ""
        speakers: []
        is_break: true
        start: "11:10:00"
        end: "11:30:00"
      - title: "45,724 Networks, One Flipper Zero: Mapping the Wireless Threat Landscape of Northern Ireland"
        description: "Nearly one in ten WiFi networks across Northern Ireland is still advertising WPS - a protocol the Wi-Fi Alliance deprecated in 2012 due to well-documented cryptographic weaknesses. Most network owners have no idea.\r\n\r\nThis talk presents the findings of an independent, passive wireless survey of 45,724 networks across Northern Ireland, conducted using commodity hardware: a Flipper Zero with an ESP32-S2 WiFi Dev Board running Marauder firmware, paired with Kismet and an Alfa adapter. No networks were accessed, probed, or interacted with - every data point was gathered from publicly broadcast beacon frames using a strictly passive methodology compliant with the Investigatory Powers Act 2016, the Wireless Telegraphy Act 2006, and the Computer Misuse Act 1990.\r\n\r\nThe numbers tell a stark story. Of the 45,724 networks observed, 4,460 (9.75%) were actively advertising WPS, and 555 (1.2%) were completely open with no encryption at all. The A26 corridor alone - a single arterial route - yielded 12,730 networks in one survey pass. Default SSIDs from major ISPs were widespread, suggesting factory configurations left untouched. Affected networks were identified near healthcare facilities, schools, legal practices, financial services offices, and government buildings.\r\n\r\nThe talk walks through three realistic attack scenarios grounded in these findings. First, WPS PIN brute-force against a healthcare-adjacent network using CVE-2011-5053 (the Viehbock half-PIN weakness that reduces the keyspace from 100 million to roughly 11,000 combinations) and CVE-2014-9494 (Pixie Dust offline recovery). Second, passive eavesdropping on open networks near educational institutions. Third, evil twin deployment targeting default-SSID networks around financial services. Each scenario is mapped to real-world precedent - from the TJX breach to the GRU close-access WiFi operations documented in the 2018 OPCW indictments.\r\n\r\nI will also present a case study from my own bug bounty research: a WPS PIN vulnerability discovered in BMW in-vehicle hotspots broadcasting persistently from production vehicles on public roads. The affected access points were detectable at over 100 metres using the same commodity hardware - more than 33 times the range BMW's own documentation states the feature should operate at. This finding, submitted through a responsible disclosure programme, demonstrates that the WPS problem extends beyond fixed infrastructure into the automotive IoT space.\r\n\r\nThe session closes with a sector-by-sector risk assessment for Northern Ireland and a set of practical, actionable recommendations: immediate steps (disable WPS, close open networks), short-term measures (network segmentation, WPA3 migration, 802.1X), and strategic changes (regulatory enforcement, public awareness). Every recommendation is grounded in the data presented.\r\n\r\nAttendees will leave with a clear understanding of how easy it is to map wireless infrastructure at scale using cheap, legal, passive tools - and why the results should concern anyone responsible for network security in Northern Ireland or similar regions. No specialist equipment or deep wireless expertise is required to follow along."
        speakers:
          - M0r3al
        is_break: false
        start: "11:30:00"
        end: "12:15:00"
      - title: "Hacking Browsers: The Easy Way"
        description: "When you think of hacking browsers, you perhaps think of V8 heap exploitation, deep-dive fuzzing, crazy sandbox escapes, and so on. But what if I told you that you can still find vulnerabilities in major browsers that don’t require any technical knowledge? Bugs you can even run into by accident!\r\nIn this talk, I’ll take you through my journey of how I “accidentally“ found a vulnerability in Google Chrome. And how that led me to find more vulnerabilities in Chrome as well as some vulnerabilities in Mozilla Firefox and many more bugs in other products.\r\nSo if you’re keen to find out how I could, with minimal user-interaction, steal your private GitHub repositories, then this talk is for you!"
        speakers:
          - Robbe Van Roey / PinkDraconian
        is_break: false
        start: "12:15:00"
        end: "13:00:00"
      - title: Lunch
        description: ""
        speakers: []
        is_break: true
        start: "13:00:00"
        end: "13:45:00"
      - title: Reverse Engineering an OAuth Supply Chain Attack
        description: "Let's reconstruct an OAuth supply chain attack.\r\n\r\nIn these attacks no one really breaks into your company. The attackers simply walk through the door, using trusted credentials you've given to one of your vendors. They are impossible to prevent. But, is there really nothing we can do about it?\r\n\r\nThe best way to figure it out is to do it yourself: so, let's create 3 organisations, give them all real infrastructure, and reverse engineer a real breach to see what allows supply chain attacks to be most effective.\r\n\r\nWe'll design potential attacks (incl. LLM-assisted attacks), run them against real-world infrastructure, and find out what makes breaking in easier (or, harder) when a trusted vendor is breached.\r\n\r\nThis talk is based on the Vercel breach in April 2026.\r\nhttps://vercel.com/kb/bulletin/vercel-april-2026-security-incident\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nEarlier this year we got a breach notification from Vercel. The kind I've had to write myself in the past. This time I was on the other end.\r\n\r\n\"We’ve identified a security incident that involved unauthorized access to certain internal Vercel systems.\"\r\n\r\nThe email said we're (probably) not affected. But, the public information they shared got me thinking about the blanks they left out.\r\n\r\n1. What really happened?\r\n2. How can the access we grant 3rd-parties as part of standard day-to-day work be abused?\r\n3. Can an upstream breach move through our company, and propagate downstream our customers?\r\n4. What is the real risk?\r\n5. Can we do anything about it?\r\n\r\nI decided to figure it out.\r\n\r\nSo, I built the infrastructure for 3 organisations using real services and realistic architectures. Then, experimented with designing ways to break in. The results were practical insights for defence - finding out what makes these attacks easy, and what makes them hard.\r\n\r\nThat's what I'd like to share in this talk, with a (hopefully) interactive demo of a (demonstrative) OAuth supply chain attack."
        speakers:
          - art
        is_break: false
        start: "13:45:00"
        end: "14:30:00"
      - title: "No CVV, No 3DS, No Problem: Chaining Trust Failures Across a Live Aviation Payment Stack"
        description: "Modern airline platforms operate across layered API architectures:\r\nairline-owned orchestration, third-party payment integrations,\r\nand external 3DS providers. Each under a different trust model.\r\nNone enforcing card ownership.\r\n\r\nThis talk documents a real engagement where a chain of broken\r\ntrust assumptions across three independent backend systems\r\nproduced a confirmed payment fraud primitive: a real booking\r\non a victim's card, no CVV, no 3DS. A second scenario converted\r\nthat access into a full financial extraction cycle. A third\r\ndemonstrates operational disruption at scale. All confirmed\r\nin production.\r\n\r\nBeyond the technical walkthrough, the talk covers how Burp Suite\r\nMCP and Claude changed triage speed without replacing operator\r\njudgment, what defenders currently cannot see in this attack\r\nsurface, and where ATT&CK and the newly published MITRE Fight\r\nFraud Framework leave practitioners without the language to\r\ndescribe what just happened.\r\n\r\nAttendees leave with a repeatable offensive methodology for\r\nAPI-first payment stacks and detection gaps they can take back\r\nto their blue teams."
        speakers:
          - Esat Berk Kandemir
        is_break: false
        start: "14:30:00"
        end: "15:15:00"
      - title: Afternoon break
        description: ""
        speakers: []
        is_break: true
        start: "15:15:00"
        end: "15:40:00"
      - title: "Chaining the Unchainable: Finding and Exploiting Logic Flaws in Modern Web Architectures"
        description: "Most scanners will never find these bugs. This talk is about what happens when you chain enough of them together anyway. Spoiler: it ends in RCE and full tenant isolation bypass.\r\n\r\nBusiness logic flaws are unglamorous. They don't have CVEs, they don't get blog posts, and individually they'll barely register as medium severity on most reports. But in the microservice-heavy, federated identity, multi-tenant environments most organisations are running today, these small mismatches compound. The interesting stuff happens at the joins, like where an API gateway hands off to a downstream service, where an event queue trusts state it shouldn't, or where OAuth flows have one assumption too many baked in.\r\n\r\nThis session walks through three attack chains pulled from real engagements. This is not sanitised theory. We will look at actual proxy logs, HTTP pairs, and the exact moment each system broke. The three paths covered include:\r\n\r\n1. Race conditions in async queues: Abusing state validation gaps in event-driven architectures to push through unauthorised transactions.\r\n\r\n2. Token propagation abuse: Exploiting the trust gap between an API gateway and its downstream services to chain into deep IDOR.\r\n\r\n3. OAuth state-machine quirks: Turning minor implementation oversights into persistent session hijacking.\r\n\r\nThe goal isn't just to show what broke. Attendees will come away with a method for reading an architecture diagram and spotting where the logic synchronisation is weakest, a framework for building these chains deliberately, and concrete design-layer fixes that actually address the root cause rather than just patching symptoms.\r\n\r\nKey Takeaways for Attendees:\r\n\r\n- A clear method for analyzing an architecture diagram to spot where logic synchronization is most vulnerable.\r\n\r\n- An adversarial framework for systematically building exploit chains out of low-severity context flaws.\r\n\r\n- Concrete, design-layer remediation strategies that address the root cause of these architectural gaps."
        speakers:
          - Raj Dhruv
        is_break: false
        start: "15:40:00"
        end: "16:25:00"
      - title: "Leaks on the Livewire: How a Few Blocked Requests Led to a Shocking Discovery"
        description: "What started as unremarkable PHP deserialization attacks blocked by our Cloud WAF unravelled to reveal a sprawling credential theft operation. This talk walks through the full lifecycle of our investigation into exploit attempts against CVE-2025-54068 (a critical unauthenticated RCE in Laravel Livewire v3), from initial detection to recovery and analysis of the attacker's exfiltration infrastructure.\r\n\r\nWe'll cover the vulnerability mechanics, the PHP gadget chains used to exploit it, and the credential-stealing malware deployed post-exploitation. The real story, however, lies in what came next: gaining access to the attacker's C2 infrastructure and analysing over 21,000 stolen Laravel .env files.\r\n\r\nA single .env file contains the keys to an entire application: database credentials, payment processor secrets, cloud access keys, and encryption keys. The scale of what we found across thousands of compromised applications may surprise you, as will the OSINT trail that led us to the threat actor. We close with practical detection and mitigation guidance and a clear-eyed look at what .env exposure really means for an organisation."
        speakers:
          - Daniel Johnston
        is_break: false
        start: "16:25:00"
        end: "17:10:00"
      - title: Evening Keynote
        description: The closing keynote session.
        speakers:
          - Aleksandra Aytova
        is_break: false
        start: "17:10:00"
        end: "17:50:00"
      - title: Closing Remarks
        description: ""
        speakers: []
        is_break: true
        start: "17:50:00"
