        end: "23:00:00"
  Track 2 - Library Bar:
    sessions:
      - title: QUICly Changing Network Comms
        description: "As defenses become more proactive and less reactive, the days of reliable unfettered egress to the internet are drawing to a close.  At the same time, the increasing reliance on third parties necessitates increasing the size of the egress whitelist.  The combination of the two requires a shift in C2 comms from relatively direct connections to infrastructure to proxying data via whitelisted services.  Unfortunately, however, whitelisted services are often somewhat reluctant to get involved in even a simple shell-over-TLS connection, instead insisting on REST APIs, bespoke protocols, or worse.\r\n\r\nFortunately for those of us who really would like to receive encrypted shell output, it turns out all we really need is something which can transport discrete messages from compromised hosts back to our own infrastructure, at the cost of having to deal with session management, timeouts, in-order delivery, and all of the other fiddly details TCP and TLS solve.  Even more fortunately, there's a protocol which translates discrete messages to something TCPish: QUIC.\r\n\r\nWe'll take a look at (ab)using QUIC, the protocol underlying HTTP/3, to take care of some of the complexity of sending a TLS stream over nearly anything that can shuffle messages between our shell and our server.  We'll first take a high-level look at QUIC itself, drawing parallels to TCP and to SSH, and then we'll take a look at the fun bits of a simple implant using shell I/O over TLS over QUIC over SaaS.\r\n\r\nWhile the talk will make use of the programming language [Go](https://go.dev), no prior experience with Go will be assumed.  Some familiarity with programming will be of benefit to understand the finer details of the talk, but no programming background will be necessary to benefit from the overarching concepts.\r\n"
        speakers:
          - Stuart McMurray
        is_break: false
        start: "10:25:00"
        end: "11:10:00"
      - title: "OWASP PTK: Security Testing Where the Browser Is the Source of Truth"
        description: "Modern web applications are no longer simple request-and-response systems. They are authenticated, JavaScript-heavy, stateful, SPA-driven, and full of behaviour that only appears after a real user follows the right path through the browser. Traditional scanners and proxy-based workflows are powerful, but they can lose important context: the current user session, DOM state, client-side routes, tokens, storage, runtime JavaScript behaviour, and the exact sequence of actions that produced a finding.\r\n\r\nThis talk introduces OWASP PenTest Kit, a browser-native application security toolkit designed to bring security testing closer to where modern applications actually run. I will explain why PTK exists, the problems it tries to solve, and how browser-based testing can complement established AppSec workflows rather than replace them.\r\n\r\nThe session will cover practical PTK workflows across DAST, SAST, IAST, and browser-driven scanning, showing how testing from inside the browser can help with authenticated flows, single-page applications, client-side code, request inspection, tokens, cookies, and live application behaviour. I will also cover how PTK integrates with OWASP ZAP, combining ZAP’s proxy and traffic visibility with PTK’s in-browser analysis and testing capabilities.\r\n\r\nThe second half of the talk focuses on automation. I will show how PTK can be used beyond manual testing through its npm package, crawler-driven workflows, and support for agent-assisted approaches using tools such as OpenCode and Codex-style automation. The goal is to demonstrate how browser-native testing, crawling, automation, and existing proxy tooling can work together in a repeatable AppSec workflow.\r\n\r\nAttendees will leave with a practical understanding of where OWASP PTK fits, what problems it is designed to solve, how it can support manual and automated testing, and how browser-native security tooling can improve coverage for modern web applications."
        speakers:
          - Denis Podgurskii
        is_break: false
        start: "11:30:00"
        end: "12:15:00"
      - title: "From Code to Coverage: A Detection Engineer's Journey Through the LDAP Wilderness"
        description: "Active Directory reconnaissance tools like BloodHound, Impacket, and SOAPHound are the attacker's first move in enterprise compromises, yet detecting their LDAP queries remains one of the hardest problems in security operations. This talk chronicles a six month journey from writing my first broken Sigma rule to building a complete, evasion resistant LDAP detection stack.\r\n\r\nYou'll learn why traditional signature based detection fails spectacularly, how to think like both an attacker and a parser, and how mathematical approaches can outsmart evasion techniques. We'll cover OID transformations that break your rules, whitespace variations that mock your regex, hidden LDAP parameters that bypass your detections, and ultimately, statistical methods that make evasion mathematically impossible.\r\n\r\nThis isn't theory. Every technique is battle tested in production environments with working Sigma rules, real attack logs, and actual false positive rates. Leave with detection rules you can deploy Monday morning."
        speakers:
          - Andrew Schwartz
        is_break: false
        start: "12:15:00"
        end: "13:00:00"
      - title: "Post Quantum Cryptography: An Introduction in Plaintext"
        description: "With the UK National Cyber Security Centre setting out timelines for migration to post-quantum cryptography - including completing discovery and planning by 2028 - the quantum future is no longer theoretical. \r\n\r\nQuantum computing may still seem abstract, but its impact on classical cryptography is real. This talk explains the mathematics behind today’s encryption, how quantum computing threatens it, why data stolen today could be decrypted tomorrow, and how your organisation can start preparing now."
        speakers:
          - Lucy Bell
        is_break: false
        start: "13:45:00"
        end: "14:30:00"
      - title: "Building a digital beehive: The cluster that wasn't real, but the attacks were"
        description: "Kubernetes is everywhere now—and so are attacks against it. Misconfigurations, exposed APIs, and overly permissive RBAC have made clusters a prime target, while most security tools stop at scanning images and YAML instead of showing what attackers do after they get in.\r\n\r\nThis talk introduces KubeDecoy – a lightweight, open-source Kubernetes honeypot built from familiar components: vcluster, Falco, Falcosidekick, and NGINX. The goal is simple: stand up a convincing fake cluster, expose realistic attack surfaces, and quietly observe how real adversaries interact with them.\r\n\r\nWe’ll walk through the architecture, a live-style deployment on Minikube, and practical workarounds for vcluster’s limitations using only open-source components. Along the way, we’ll map KubeDecoy’s detections to the Kubernetes Threat Matrix, highlight where it shines (and where it doesn’t), and show how you can apply these ideas in your own clusters—whether you’re blue team, red team, or somewhere in between."
        speakers:
          - Arnav Tripathy
        is_break: false
        start: "14:30:00"
        end: "15:15:00"
      - title: "Bedtime Bug Hunting: A Friendly Android Auth Bypass Story"
        description: "One night, while lying in bed and idly poking around an Android app for a membership organisation I belong to, I found myself somewhere I did not expect to be: past the login flow.\r\nThis lightning talk is a short, friendly story about curiosity, mobile apps, and responsible disclosure. It is not about shaming an organisation or showing off a live exploit. In fact, once the issue was reported, the organisation responded quickly and the app was patched soon after. That is the kind of outcome we should want to see more often.\r\nThe talk will cover the discovery at a high level: what caught my attention, how I tested the behaviour safely, what the app appeared to trust, and why authentication and authorisation need to be enforced server-side rather than relying on client-side flows or assumptions. I will also talk about where I stopped, what I avoided doing, how I documented the finding, and how I approached reporting it constructively.\r\nThe broader theme is the value of a playful security mindset. Many useful findings start with the simple question: “What happens if I press this?” Makers, engineers, testers, and defenders all benefit from gently poking at systems, noticing odd behaviour, and then handling what they find with care.\r\nAttendees will come away with a better sense of how mobile authentication flows can go wrong, what good responsible disclosure can look like, and why curiosity plus ethics is such a useful combination in security."
        speakers:
          - Jeffrey Roe
        is_break: false
        start: "15:40:00"
        end: "16:00:00"
      - title: "The Silent Risk in Your Browser: Detecting Malicious Extensions"
        description: "Browser extensions are used by millions of users and professionals inside of organizations, some for small tasks and others for more complex tasks and yet it's often ignored when it comes to security. Majority of organisations put all focus on the major points of infrastructure and overlook browser extensions. \r\n\r\nMillions of users are affected every year by malicious browser extensions, as most of the times it is the trusted ones that turn malicious after a developer gets compromised or the extension was sold. \r\n\r\nI will dive into the behaviour of known malicious extensions and best practices when it comes to handling browser extensions. Get ready to learn about behaviours you probably did not know was possible."
        speakers:
          - Gherardo Buio Fiori
        is_break: false
        start: "16:00:00"
        end: "16:25:00"
      - title: Communicating with QR Codes
        description: "We’ve all scanned a QR code to read a restaurant menu. But what if that menu was secret information that the restaurant was supposed to be hiding from us?\r\n\r\nI’ll demonstrate some ways to use QR Codes for sending data between air-gapped devices. We’ll consider out-of-band communication for exfiltrating and securing systems.\r\n\r\nThere’ll be lots of demos, and more than you wanted to know about QR codes."
        speakers:
          - Ben Foxall
        is_break: false
        start: "16:25:00"
        end: "17:10:00"
  Workshops - Penthouse:
    sessions:
      - title: Mobile Application Penetration Testing 101
        description: "Mobile Applications are some of the most widely used pieces of technology by people. However, one side of penetration testing that isn't as well known is mobile application penetration testing. \r\n\r\nIn this workshop students will be shown how to setup and proxy an android emulator, how to reverse engineer an Android application, how to bypass certificate pinning, and some basic tests they can perform against the application."
        speakers:
          - Niall Caffrey
        is_break: false
        start: "11:30:00"
        end: "13:00:00"
      - title: "Inside the CSIRT: A Collaborative Live Incident Response Workshop"
        description: "Incident response frameworks tell us what responders should do, but how do security practitioners actually investigate and respond to an attack when working under pressure?\r\n\r\nThis handson workshop places participants inside a realistic simulated ransomware incident, working as part of a Computer Security Incident Response Team (CSIRT). In small groups, attendees will investigate, contain, and respond to an attack using open source incident response tools including TheHive (Case Management), MISP (Threat Intelligence), Cortex (Enrichment), and Mattermost (Communication).\r\n\r\nFollowing a guided introduction and training session, participants will be presented with a realistic ransomware scenario. Teams will analyse indicators of compromise, document response activities, share threat intelligence, coordinate decisions, and manage the incident through the phases of the NIST Incident Response lifecycle. Participants will work collaboratively while following their own investigative workflow and response strategy. \r\n\r\n Participants will:\r\n-Learn the fundamentals of incident management using TheHive, MISP, Cortex, and Mattermost. \r\n-Experience a realistic ransomware investigation from initial detection through analysis, containment, eradication, recovery, and post-incident activities. \r\n-Practise documenting actions using the NIST Incident Response framework. \r\n-Analyse indicators of compromise using threat intelligence and enrichment tools. \r\n-Collaborate with team members to make critical response decisions throughout the incident lifecycle. \r\n\r\n\r\nNo prior experience with TheHive, MISP, or Cortex is required. The workshop is suitable for students, SOC analysts, blue team practitioners, incident responders, and anyone interested in cybersecurity operations. Attendees will leave with practical experience using modern incident response platforms, a deeper understanding of ransomware response workflows, and insight into how incident responders make decisions during real world investigations. Unlike traditional training exercises, this workshop also contributes to an ongoing cybersecurity research study exploring how different responders approach the same incident."
        speakers:
          - Haula Galadima
        is_break: false
        start: "13:45:00"
        end: "15:15:00"
      - title: "Hands-On Threat Modelling: A Practical Workshop to Secure Your Product"
        description: "Shift from reactive security to proactive defence in this hands-on threat modelling workshop. You won't just hear about threat modelling; you'll do it. We'll guide you step-by-step through deconstructing a system, identifying potential threats, documenting your findings, and brainstorming effective mitigations. Learn a practical, repeatable process based on real-world application, empowering you to uncover vulnerabilities before attackers do. Walk away with the skills and confidence to implement threat modelling for your own products, fostering a stronger security posture. Ideal for engineers, architects, and PMs who want to build more secure software through practical application."
        speakers:
          - Adam
          - Maya McDevitt
        is_break: false
        start: "15:40:00"
        end: "17:10:00"
